==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|comicalle}}, from {{der|en|la|cōmicus}} + {{der|en|enm|-al|-alle}} (modern {{m|en|-al}}).<ref>{{R:MED Online|pos=''adj.'' and ''n.''|noformat=1|id=4554983735}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈkɒmɪkəl/|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈkɑmɪkəl/|a=GenAm}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-comical.ogg|a=US}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# {{lb|en|archaic}} Originally, relating to [[comedy]].
#: {{ux|en|It was a '''comical''' performance.}}
# [[funny|Funny]], whimsically amusing.
#: {{ux|en|The tutor excelled in '''comical''' scoldings.}}
# [[laughable|Laughable]]; [[ridiculous]].
#: {{ux|en|He's just put salt in his tea instead of sugar. What a '''comical''' error!}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=January 30 2016|title=Hillary Clinton for the Democratic Nomination|titleurl=http://www.nytimes.com/2016/01/31/opinion/sunday/hillary-clinton-endorsement.html?smid=fb-nytopinion&smtyp=cur&_r=0|journal=The New York Times|accessdate=30 January 2016
|passage=Mr. Sanders has scored some rhetorical points against Mrs. Clinton for her longstanding ties to Wall Street, but she has responded well, and it would be '''comical''' to watch any of the Republican candidates try to make that case, given that they are all virtually tied to, or actually part of, the business establishment.}}

